Subject: ECON/CRYPT: Crypto-Extortion

Extropian friends,

The subject of crypto-extortion in a world with aspects of crypto-anarchy
has indeed been discussed before, and not just on the Cypherpunks list. I
mentioned that Robin Hanson, Dean Tribble, others, and I had discussed it
here on this list, I thought, because Robin was not and has not been on the
Cypherpunks list.

Here's one of my posts from March of this year, dealing with some issues
raised by Robin. There were a number of back-and-forth points, and
interested folks should go back to their own archives. I can, presumably,
post my _own_ comments (even if they contain snippets of other people's
posts, as we seem to have established by customary law), but I cannot post
other people's posts without getting all the permissions. Consider this a
reason to think again about the policy on archives and actually reach a
decision and then implement it!

I understand that people who were on the list back then (Burns, Friedman,
Zahn, Szabo, etc.) may not have been interested in crypto at that time, and
that for whatever reason, they are now interested in these issues of
crypto-extortion, digital money, crypto-anarchy, etc.

In my various comments on this issue, I'm sorry if I sound a bit abrupt and
impatient (even though I have spent far too much time writing on this
recently!). My frustration is rather high when people skip the debate the
first couple of times around, and don't join the Cypherpunks list, or read
sci.crypt, and then have a "revelation" that crypto-anarchy is an
interesting or problematic topic, or wish to write a paper for publication,
and then expect some of us to rehash arguments.

(Note that Nick Szabo and Derek Zahn have recently joined the Cypherpunks
list and have made some valuable contributions, so I am not faulting them.)

Sorry, but I don't do paper research for others unless I get some of the credit.

Here's part of the debate on crypto-extortion that appeared on this list.

Other debates occurred on the Cypherpunks list, of course.


