Date: Wed, 06 Oct 93 22:21:08 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: BLACKNET/CRYPTO: TLAs UK listening posts

Here's a few notes I made on the Channel Four programme, shown tonight on
British TV regarding the (TLA deleted)'s listening station at Menwith Hill,
Yorkshire, and others.  It showed how lax security is (was).  I wonder how
the home bases compare.  One woman had crossed the perimeter fence and got
in at least 500 times.  (Dogs were on patrol, but were too friendly to
deter.)  Films were shot of inside the buildings, and documents taken, at
various levels of classification.  Inside the base were "more than 250
surveillance systems".  Of the whole system, the most expensive parts were
the VORTEX satellites, around $1 billion each.  SIGINT systems connect
between space satellites and Silkworth computers on the ground.  (Not sure
if these names are correct.)

The Yorkshire base is a 562 acre site, a 5.5 mile perimeter.  The
satellite tracking dishes are hidden inside what look like "golf balls",
so it's impossible for outsiders to see which satellites are being tracked.
The TLAs have to find new targets or face cuts, but it seems that they've 
avoided the cuts.  30 new projects are due to be installed in the next 2
years in the Yorkshire station alone, with the SteepleBush II new satellite
ground terminal completed by May '94.  It was also mentioned that Lockheed
has an involvement in running the centre.  

Other bases are in Cornwall, Bedfordshire, Cambridgeshire, Cheltenham
(GCHQ) etc.  It was said that "huge computers" select the messages the
TLAs want to hear.  "Thousands" of recording tapes are shipped each month.
A lot of business involves commercial secrets on foreign rivals.

At one point they did say that quite a lot of constitutional laws were
broken and gave an example of a US Senator's calls being intercepted,
although it was pointed out that the TLA enjoyed "total unaccountability".

