Date: Wed, 6 Oct 1993 21:59:00 -0400
From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: CRYPTO: Brand name on criminal markets

This discussion recently raging seems to be mostly concerned with blackmail
and extortion. While those may be interesting issues, they are not my chief
concern.

What worries me is that widespread use of public key cryptography and
anonymous remailers have the potential for permitting criminal enterprises
to take advantage of economies of scale and brand name. Consider pay
killing, for example. My impression is that the current market price is not
very high--somewhere in the $10,000-$20,000 range. The real difficulty, for
most potential customers, is that it is hard and risky to find a reliable
seller of the service.

A firm interested in going into business establishes its reputation, as
various people have suggested in related contexts, by sending the papers a
description of a very newsworthy murder (encoded with their private key) 
immediately before committing it, then widely publicizing the public key.
Now many millions of people know that a message encoded with the public key
can only be read by that firm and a message that makes sense when decoded
with the public key must have been sent by that firm. The latter fact
(digital signature) can be used to announce communication protocols.

I am not arguing that nothing can be done about such firms--one can imagine
ways of trying to create fake ones to trap customers, or trying to block
the firm's mailboxes. But the new technology makes it a lot easier to
create such firms. The result should be to greatly decrease cost and
increase availability for those services for which either previously
unavailable scale economies or large transaction costs used to be a major
problem. Killing and stealing trade secrets seem obvious candidates.

David Friedman

