Indeed, use of untraceable escrow services will make the market for
killings-for-hire much more liquid, so to speak. 

Most contract killings arranged by "amateurs" (angry or greedy
spouses, business partners, etc.) are foiled by one party in the 2-way
transaction being in law enforcement. Alice wants Bob killed, asks
around a bit in seedly places, and is put in touch with Charles, who
makes a deal and then arrests her as soon as money changes hands. 

Pros rarely get zapped this way, because they don't just "ask around"
for the hits. They use other "families," in other cities, who send in
killers in fairly untraceable ways and no face-to-face negotiations
are done. (I'm simplifying, and may have details from dozens of movies
mixed in. But books and articles I've read on mob killings and CIA
methods bear this out.)

With untraceable communication and "trustworthy" escrow agents--whose
true name and identity is unknown of course--potential sellers and
potential buyers need never meet, need have no idea of who the other
party is, etc.

The escrow agent only pays if the murder (or other action) is
completed, with proof being by a "widely witnessed event," such as a
newsaper article on the killing (this is is spoofable in theory, but
in practice it's fairly evident when someone dies).

The FBI may in fact step up efforts to nab contractors by setting up
victims, then nabbing the would-be assassins. But this only alters the
market price a bit.

> A firm interested in going into business establishes its reputation, as
> various people have suggested in related contexts, by sending the papers a
> description of a very newsworthy murder (encoded with their private key) 
> immediately before committing it, then widely publicizing the public key.
> Now many millions of people know that a message encoded with the public key
> can only be read by that firm and a message that makes sense when decoded
> with the public key must have been sent by that firm. The latter fact
> (digital signature) can be used to announce communication protocols.

Yep, that's the way "Murder Incorporated" will gain a powerful rep.
But I suspect there will be a lot of small freelancers, too.

And the beauty of the escrow agency approach is that no payment is
made until the deed is done.

(The escrow agent could "burn" it customers, as any bank can
currently, but it has little to gain from this and everything to lose,
namely, its "reputation capital," to use Dean Tribble's term.)

